rong with disembodied legs and  children with sewn shut eyes anyhow  isn't that basically their slogan  filming took over three years hailing  from before the days of slick CGI the  effort that went into making the  Nightmare Before Christmas was intense  the film was shot at 24 frames per  second which meant that the characters  had to be repositioned 24 times for  every second of film it took a whole  week to create just a single minute of  footage and eight camera crews worked at  once it wasn't just a case of nudging a  figurine slightly either the characters  had to be carefully disassembled and  reassembled for every frame so the  facial expressions matched what was  happening on-screen the pumpkin King had  more than 400 interchangeable heads  alone boogy boogy was trouble despite  being a burlap sack filled with creepy  crawlies nightmares antagonist Oogie  Boogie was actually the hardest  character to create his complex demise  in the film was particularly tricky to  navigate consisting of four shots that  each took a full month to make not only  that but the metal armature inside the  huge figure was so strong that animators  had to get behind it and push it into  place for each shot says no wonder he's  barely in the film Santa Claus huh some  viewers also felt that Kim pages  portrayal of the villain burj don racist  Selleck defended the character insisting  the page modeled his voice performance  on Cab Calloway this argument is  supported by the fact that some lines of  dialogue are lifted directly from the  1933 Betty Boop cartoon the Old Man of  the Mountain star in Callaway gonna do  the best I can  what are you going to do I'm gonna do  the best I can composer Danny Elfman has  a long history of channeling Calloway as  well as he did in the 1980 film  Forbidden Zone  let me tell you I'm so pleased to meet  you  the boys and I brothers better to greet  you best friends for never all of Jack  Skellington songs were composed and sung  by Engel blingo 